  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I built the crate pyramid and wrecking ball scene in `src/main.ts` and `src/physics.ts`. It typechecks, but I couldn't run it, so I haven't seen it render and I haven't tested the physics, including whether the pyramid stands without collapsing.\n\n- **Scene:** a concrete yard with slab seams and a back wall, a gradient sky, and a soft key light with shadows. 15 wooden crates sit in a 5-4-3-2-1 pyramid. A crane (base, mast, jib, brace) holds a steel ball on a rope with a pivot at (-2, 6.9) and a length of 6. The camera frames both the crane and the stack.\n- **Physics:** I wrote my own rigid-body solver rather than use the engine's, because I wasn't sure its stacking would be stable. It runs at a fixed 1/240 s step. It handles gravity, friction, restitution, and the ground. Crates settle into sleep so the stack stays put at rest. The ball is a mass-20 sphere held at a fixed rope length (about 7.7 m/s at the bottom of the swing).\n- **UI:** the Swing button pulls the ball back to 60° over 0.6 s, then releases it from rest. Pressing Swing again re-swings without rebuilding the stack. The Reset button rebuilds the pyramid and hangs the ball at rest.\n- **State:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is a read-only getter, updated every frame, with `phase`, `crates` (always 15), `displaced` (crates moved more than 0.75 from their start) and `ballAngleDeg`. `ballAngleDeg` is the absolute angle from vertical. Phase is `settled` once the ball has been released for more than 2.5 s and every crate is asleep.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first frame.\n\nThe collision code only generates contacts where a corner of one crate lies inside another, so an edge-on-edge contact between two tumbling crates would be missed.\n\n",
